#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147326 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b114a373-697f-5005-bb1b-46202547a905" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying the Equilibrium Points of a Given Differential Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "eed50b24-e97c-51c9-b625-3327783b6374" :question/math-academy-id "e-8536" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of $y'= y^3-2y^2$?\n\n1. $y=0$ is a source, and $y=2$ is a node\n2. $y=0$ is a node, and $y=2$ is a source\n3. $y=0$ is a node, and $y=2$ is a sink" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE $y' = f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve\n\n$$\nf(y)=0\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(y) &= 0 \\\\\ny^{3} - 2y^{2} &= 0 \\\\\ny^{2}(y - 2) &= 0 \\\\\ny &= 0, 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=0$ and $y=2$.\n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y^2$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $y-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(y)$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (2,\\infty)$, and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty,0) \\cup (0,2)$.\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2533/Source/Images/df13c830b5ad2820b96b71562262e470.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=0$ is a node because it is neither a source nor a sink.\n- $y=2$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=0$ is a node, and $y=2$ is a source\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147327 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b58b04cc-da5e-5c51-a583-0663d783c697" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Slope Fields Corresponding to Some Equilibrium Point Classifications"}] :question/id #uuid "dba1deed-4311-54fe-a690-8d89d4cb9592" :question/math-academy-id "e-8537" :question/problem "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y= -2$ is a source\n- $y= 3$ is a sink\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2533/Source/Images/44ac32155e0ac94909f9f9fb1422161a.png)" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE $y' = f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information.\n\nNote that there are $2$ equilibrium points, $y=-2$ and $y=3$.\n\n- We're given that $y=-2$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=-2$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n- We're given that $y=3$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=3$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it (already drawn by the above property).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2533/Source/Images/c28659ee667c96e5bc9ae89eec4f5117.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2533/Source/Images/98572c7a989e66218338b945945a9fd4.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-2$ and $y=3$.\n- The slopes near $y=-2$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n- The slopes near $y=3$ appear to converge toward this line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147328 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4535b48e-a33f-5f62-95d3-a5c0991c1f6d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "7aa0a0a8-05d4-532c-a830-f1df43514fdf" :question/math-academy-id "e-8538" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2533/Source/Images/0c3476e4101868035cc925ba79320c52.png)\n\n1. $y= -3$ is a sink.\n2. $y= -1$ is a node.\n3. $y= 2$ is a source." :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE $y' = f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2533/Source/Images/8026555a0afe7e99a825261eb4f98249.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=-3$ is a source.\n- Statement II is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=-1$ is a sink.\n- Statement III is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=2$ is a source.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147330 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "680a42fd-9b70-5fb2-916c-d10b71b11d82" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using Phase Lines"}] :question/id #uuid "bb252f4f-ef82-52bc-a485-f88f0de96173" :question/math-academy-id "e-8650" :question/problem "A particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2533/Source/Images/0a84b8805b21add477b7c6107be73de4.png)\n\n1. $y=1$ is a source\n2. $y=-2$ is a sink\n3. $y=-2$ is a node" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE $y' = f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=1$ points upward, and the arrow just below it points downward. In other words, both arrows point *away* from $y=1$. This means that $y = 1$ is a source.\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=-2$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points downward too. In other words, both arrows point in the *same* direction on either side of $y=-2$. This means that $y=-2$ is a node.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "680a42fd-9b70-5fb2-916c-d10b71b11d82" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using Phase Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9328e16e-9916-52b9-bd60-693348c5eda2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147026} {:answer/id #uuid "dfab5c44-428b-5c0c-a0ce-428531b48567" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147027} {:answer/id #uuid "0bb78471-e403-5061-aa64-a3e8fb8c2ca5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147028} {:answer/id #uuid "46abd4d9-c693-5489-b647-763bf2aabe19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147029} {:answer/id #uuid "71ef255f-2d94-59f3-a224-e8011f0f15ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46abd4d9-c693-5489-b647-763bf2aabe19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147029} :answer-field/id #uuid "48c22f42-8a2f-5b9a-b085-80bd413e8101" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147025}] :question/id #uuid "4de3adcb-e14e-526f-9d74-b55f67fd1c9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-96558" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2533/Source/Images/q-96558.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y =-1$ is a source\n2. $y = 2$ is a sink\n3. $y = 2$ is a node"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148175 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4535b48e-a33f-5f62-95d3-a5c0991c1f6d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using a Graph"}] 